  <title xml:lang="EN">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Niger Second Laying the Foundation for Inclusive Development Policy Financing</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2" xml:lang="EN">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This document proposes a Development Policy Financing (DPF) that supports Niger&apos;s response plan to mitigate the impact of the COVID-19 while sowing the seeds for resilience and faster economic recovery.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1" xml:lang="EN">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This document proposes a Development Policy Financing (DPF) that supports Niger&apos;s response plan to mitigate the impact of the COVID19 pandemic while sowing the seeds for resilience and faster  economic  recovery.  This is the  second  and  last  operation  in  a  single-tranche  disbursement  of  a  programmatic DPF series to be disbursed in calendar year 2020. The proposed operation was adjusted to consider COVID-19  emerging  needs.  It has  three  pillars:  (i)  reducing  gender  gaps  and  protecting  livelihoods; (ii) expanding access to electricity and potable water; and; (iii) improving debt transparency  and  debt management  capacity.  These  pillars  are  consistent  with  the WBG  COVID-19  Crisis  Response  Approach Paper and with the pillars of the FY18-FY22 CPF for Niger. The proposed operation combines an  International Development Association (IDA) Grant of US125 million dollars equivalent on standard IDA Grant  terms, an IDA Credit of US125 million dollars equivalent on standard IDA regular credit terms with maturity of  38  years  including  a  grace  period  of  six  years.  The  first  operation  amounting  to  US350  million dollars  was  approved by the Board in December 2019. Niger&apos;s macroeconomic policy  framework is adequate  for  the proposed operation. The post- COVID-19 medium-term economic outlook shows a rebound from 2021 onwards even under a downside  scenario. Non-resource GDP will lead growth supported by investment in infrastructure (ICT, electricity)  and  agricultural  productivity.  The  advent  of  crude  oil exports  in  2022  will  provide  further  boost  to  growth and exports. Building on satisfactory performance under the International Monetary Fund (IMF)  Extended Credit Facility  (ECF) since 2017 and its Rapid Credit Facility  (RCF) of SDR 86.3 million  to help  cover estimated external and fiscal financing gaps brought by the COVID-19 pandemic the macro policy  framework is underpinned  by  the  continued implementation of  sound macroeconomic policies. These  policies support debt sustainability and macroeconomic stability and might be anchored in the current  ECF extended beyond its expiration at end-July 2020 or by a new ECF program. Niger&apos;s membership in the  West African Economic and Monetary Union (WAEMU) also offers an anchor of the macro framework. It  is in this context that Niger would reach the WAEMU  fiscal target in 2022 and the risk of external and  overall public debt distress remain moderate.</narrative>
  </description>
